= Belief bias =

== Overview ==
'''Belief bias''' is the tendency for the believability of a conclusion to influence evaluation of whether an argument is logically valid.

== Logic and content ==
In syllogistic reasoning experiments, participants can be more likely to accept invalid arguments with believable conclusions or reject valid arguments with unbelievable conclusions.

== Interpretation ==
Belief bias demonstrates interaction between semantic knowledge and formal reasoning. It should not be equated with [[Confirmation bias]], which concerns preferential treatment of information relative to existing beliefs more broadly.

== Artificial systems ==
Language models can also show content effects in reasoning: logically equivalent structures may be handled differently when conclusions agree or conflict with learned semantic expectations.

== References ==
* Evans, Barston & Pollard, “On the Conflict Between Logic and Belief in Syllogistic Reasoning”, ''Memory & Cognition'' 11 (1983): 295–306.
* Dasgupta et al., “Language models show human-like content effects on reasoning tasks”, 2022.

